# DNA-AND content · the grand tour: one flight from the whole visible universe down to the quarks, for watching
# Run it: Universe.exe --tour grand-tour (or DNA-AND.exe --tour grand-tour). Any key or click takes the controls back.
# Edit the stops to make your own. Each @stop is done in order:
#   do: zoom    fly in (or out, with a negative rate) steadily until the view is "until-km" across (km, the distance to what
#               you look at); rate: tenfolds a second (one steady rate, each tenfold the same time), eased in and out; a zoom
#               that stops getting anywhere for six seconds goes on to the next stop
#   do: orbit   circle slowly round what is picked (or look round if nothing is): turn degrees a second, for seconds; the view
#               is eased toward level first, so it never spins round straight up or down
#   do: look    tilt your view up (+) or down (-) by tilt degrees over seconds, turning a little as it goes
#   do: arc     the cinematic move: a slow dolly in (rate, until-km as zoom) while circling (turn) for seconds, eased
#   do: fly     fly steadily forward past what is ahead for seconds: rate is the share of the distance to the nearest thing
#               a second (no sudden speed-ups), turn degrees a second as it goes
#   do: glide   fly low over the land, level at the height you are at, rising and falling with the ground: rate is heights a
#               second (at head height, a walk; from higher, a glide over the hills), looking a little ahead and down,
#               turning turn degrees a second, for seconds
#   do: pick    choose what to go to: galaxy, next-galaxy, star, planet, next-planet, none, atom, bonded-atom (the next atom,
#               sharing its outer electrons: the camera glides across the bond), electron (each time the next electron, not
#               the one you are on), nucleus, proton, quark
#   caption:    the words shown while the stop runs
# It is told as a story: each stop stays until its words have had time to be read (a moment to look, then about a
# third of a second a word); a zoom that arrives early holds still. It plays as a film: letterbox bars, captions fading in and out, the camera easing round to each new subject, holds.
# Picks explore: a galaxy chosen afresh some way off, a star among those round you, one of the star's worlds, so every
# seed and every run is its own journey.
# Every caption is followed by how far the view reaches at that moment: the scale is what the tour shows.
# For the eye: zoom about one tenfold in ten seconds, turn no more than two degrees a second, and stop to look round at
# each new scale, so each size can be read against the last. Nothing goes by at warp speed.
# Sizes for scale: the visible universe 1.3e23 km, a web filament 1e21 km, a galaxy 1e18 km, a star system 1e10 km, Earth
# 12,742 km, a person 0.0018 km, a sand grain 2e-7 km, an atom 2e-13 km, a nucleus 1e-17 km, a proton 1.7e-18 km.

@stop leave
do: zoom
rate: -0.1
until-km: 5e21
caption: We begin as far out as anything can see. Every point of light here is a whole galaxy

@stop web
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 30
caption: This is the cosmic web: galaxies strung along filaments and walls, gathered where they cross, round voids hundreds of millions of light-years across

@stop web-look
do: look
tilt: 15
turn: 1
seconds: 12
caption: Gravity drew it out of tiny ripples in the young universe, over thirteen billion years

@stop back-in
do: zoom
rate: 0.12
until-km: 3e19
caption: Now we fall toward one strand of the web

@stop choose-galaxy
do: pick
target: galaxy
caption: A galaxy

@stop to-galaxy
do: zoom
rate: 0.1
until-km: 1.5e18
caption: One galaxy among a hundred billion

@stop round-galaxy
do: arc
rate: 0.03
until-km: 6e17
turn: 2
seconds: 32
caption: A hundred billion stars or more, turning once in a few hundred million years, round a black hole millions of times the Sun's mass

@stop choose-next
do: pick
target: next-galaxy
caption: On to a neighbour

@stop to-next
do: zoom
rate: 0.12
until-km: 1.5e18
caption: Galaxies come in groups. Its neighbour lies millions of light-years away, the emptiest space there is

@stop round-next
do: arc
rate: 0.03
until-km: 6e17
turn: 2
seconds: 26
caption: No two galaxies are alike: spirals with arms of young blue stars, smooth ellipticals of old red ones, irregular clouds

@stop into-galaxy
do: zoom
rate: 0.1
until-km: 1e16
caption: Into the galaxy. The glow breaks into stars, each a sun

@stop among-stars
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 20
caption: Among its stars: young clusters, glowing clouds of gas where new stars are born, the light of old ones

@stop choose-star
do: pick
target: star
caption: A star

@stop into-system
do: zoom
rate: 0.1
until-km: 3e9
caption: One star, and the worlds that formed round it from the same cloud

@stop system-above
do: look
tilt: -25
turn: 1.5
seconds: 14
caption: Its planets go round it on orbits of their own, each tilted a little from the rest

@stop round-system
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 30
caption: Rocky worlds close in, giants of gas and ice farther out where it is cold, belts of rubble between

@stop past-star
do: fly
rate: 0.12
turn: 1.5
seconds: 22
caption: Past the star itself: a ball of hydrogen turning into helium in its heart, the fire that lights every world here

@stop planet-1
do: pick
target: next-planet
caption: One of its worlds

@stop to-planet-1
do: zoom
rate: 0.1
until-km: 1e5
caption: One of its worlds

@stop round-planet-1
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 22
caption: Every world is made from its place: what was near it in the cloud, how much heat its star gave it

@stop planet-2
do: pick
target: next-planet
caption: The next world

@stop to-planet-2
do: zoom
rate: 0.12
until-km: 1e5
caption: Across to the next

@stop round-planet-2
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 22
caption: Its own size, its own air or none, its own ground

@stop choose-planet
do: pick
target: planet
caption: The world we go down to

@stop to-planet
do: zoom
rate: 0.1
until-km: 4e4
caption: And down to the world we will walk on

@stop round-planet
do: arc
rate: 0.03
until-km: 1.5e4
turn: 2
seconds: 32
caption: Seas, land, clouds, a thin shell of air: from here the whole world fits in the view

@stop to-land
do: zoom
rate: 0.1
until-km: 2
caption: Down through the air

@stop over-hills
do: glide
rate: 0.4
turn: 1
seconds: 30
caption: Over the land: hills raised by the planet's slow moving plates, valleys worn down by water and wind

@stop lower
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 0.06
caption: Lower

@stop over-ground
do: glide
rate: 0.3
turn: 1.5
seconds: 24
caption: The ground: rock broken down into soil, the land's own minerals

@stop to-head-height
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 0.002
caption: Down to where a person stands

@stop stand
do: pick
target: none
caption: On the ground, six feet up

@stop walk
do: glide
rate: 0.5
turn: 1
seconds: 18
caption: Walking. Everything above us and everything below us, we stand halfway between in size

@stop look-up
do: look
tilt: 55
turn: 1.5
seconds: 14
caption: Look up

@stop sky
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 28
caption: The sky: our star, the other worlds as wandering points, the galaxy's stars behind them all. Everything we flew through

@stop look-down
do: look
tilt: -85
turn: 1
seconds: 14
caption: And now down, to the ground at our feet. There is as much below us as above

@stop to-stones
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 1e-4
caption: The ground breaks into stones

@stop round-stones
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 14
caption: Pebbles and stones, broken from the land's own rock

@stop to-grains
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 2e-6
caption: And the stones into grains of sand

@stop round-grains
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 16
caption: Each grain a crystal of one mineral, its own colour, shape and stains

@stop to-face
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 2e-9
caption: Onto one grain. It fills the whole view now

@stop round-face
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 16
caption: Its face is a landscape of its own: steps, cracks, clay plates, a skin of iron rust, a film of water

@stop to-crystal
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 6e-12
caption: Down to its atoms

@stop round-crystal
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 22
caption: The crystal: atoms in a pattern repeated billions of times, each held to its neighbours

@stop choose-atom
do: pick
target: atom
caption: One atom

@stop to-atom
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 6e-13
caption: Into one atom

@stop round-atom
do: arc
rate: 0.03
until-km: 2.5e-13
turn: 2
seconds: 30
caption: An atom is not a ball. It is a nucleus, far too small to see from here, and its electrons round it, each on its own tilted path

@stop to-neighbour
do: pick
target: bonded-atom
caption: Across to its neighbour

@stop across-bond
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 18
caption: Atoms hold together by sharing their outer electrons. This glow between them is the bond

@stop to-next-neighbour
do: pick
target: bonded-atom
caption: And on to the next atom

@stop along-lattice
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 18
caption: Atom to atom, every bond a shared pair of electrons. This is what keeps the stone solid

@stop choose-electron
do: pick
target: electron
caption: One electron

@stop follow-electron
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 16
caption: One electron: as small as anything ever measured, a point with no size at all

@stop next-electron
do: pick
target: electron
caption: On to the next electron

@stop follow-next
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 14
caption: Another. An electron is not a little planet. It is a wave, spread through its orbital

@stop third-electron
do: pick
target: electron
caption: And the next

@stop follow-third
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 14
caption: Where an electron is, only its cloud can say: most likely here, less likely there

@stop choose-nucleus
do: pick
target: nucleus
caption: The nucleus

@stop to-nucleus
do: zoom
rate: 0.12
until-km: 3e-17
caption: Now inward, through the electrons' cloud. The nucleus is a hundred thousand times smaller than the atom. Almost all of an atom is empty

@stop round-nucleus
do: arc
rate: 0.03
until-km: 1e-17
turn: 2
seconds: 26
caption: The nucleus: protons and neutrons packed tight, holding almost all the atom's mass, bound by the strong force

@stop choose-proton
do: pick
target: proton
caption: One proton

@stop to-proton
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 2.5e-18
caption: Into a single proton

@stop round-proton
do: orbit
turn: 2
seconds: 20
caption: A proton is not solid either: three quarks, two up and one down, in a storm of gluons that never rests

@stop choose-quark
do: pick
target: quark
caption: One quark

@stop to-quark
do: zoom
rate: 0.08
until-km: 6e-19
caption: And one quark

@stop end
do: orbit
turn: 1.5
seconds: 30
caption: A quark: the smallest thing we know. From the cosmic web to here, the same universe at every scale
